# Profile D: the debugging kernel, for lesson C09.
#
# lockdep watches every lock the kernel takes and builds a graph of the order they were taken in,
# then complains the first time an ordering could deadlock, whether or not it did. That is the
# whole point of C09, and it costs enough that this is not the image a reader boots by default.
#
# KASAN used to be here for the same reason, and it was never in the kernel that came out. On
# 32 bit x86 `arch/x86/Kconfig` selects HAVE_ARCH_KASAN only if X86_64, so CONFIG_KASAN is not a
# symbol on this build, and Kconfig drops a symbol whose dependencies are unmet without a word. The
# built config for this profile has no CONFIG_KASAN line at all. The only thing in it with KASAN in
# the name is CONFIG_CC_HAS_KASAN_GENERIC=y, which says the compiler could have done it.
#
# The memory checking this architecture can actually do lives in `config/memcheck.config`, which is
# built out of KFENCE and the page and slab debuggers. `tools/kconfig` now refuses a fragment that
# asks for a symbol the architecture gates away, so this cannot come back quietly.

CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_LOCK_ALLOC=y
CONFIG_LOCKDEP=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_LOCKDEP=y
CONFIG_LOCK_STAT=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_SPINLOCK=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_MUTEXES=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_RWSEMS=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_KMEMLEAK=y
CONFIG_FAULT_INJECTION=y
CONFIG_FAILSLAB=y
